<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="situation">What Was Happening</h2>
<p>Bijan Robinson and Jahmyr Gibbs both reported to their teams' training camps this summer but weren't practicing — a "hold-in" rather than a holdout, since neither stayed away from the building. Both entered the final two years of their rookie deals after Atlanta and Detroit exercised their fifth-year options, and both were seeking extensions that would make them among the league's highest-paid backs. Neither side signaled urgency to blink first: whichever player signed first would effectively set the price for the other.</p>
<p>The stakes were real money. Saquon Barkley's deal with the Eagles had set the running back ceiling at roughly $20.6 million a year. Robinson's projected market value sat around $18.87 million annually on a three-year deal, which would make him the league's third-highest-paid back behind Barkley and Christian McCaffrey. Gibbs was believed to be seeking a deal north of $20 million a season himself.</p>
<h2 id="profiles">Two Backs, Two Very Different Combine Profiles</h2>
<p>What makes the Robinson-Gibbs pairing interesting from an athletic standpoint is how differently they tested coming out in 2023 — and how closely that gap has tracked their styles on the field ever since.</p>
