<h2 id="comparison">Side-by-Side Comparison</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Measurable</th><th>Bijan Robinson</th><th>Jahmyr Gibbs</th></tr>
<tr><td>Height / Weight</td><td>5'11", 215 lbs</td><td>5'9", 199 lbs</td></tr>
<tr><td>40-Yard Dash</td><td>4.46s</td><td>4.36s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vertical Jump</td><td>37"</td><td>33.5"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broad Jump</td><td>10'4"</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>10-Yard Split</td><td>1.52s</td><td>1.52s</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Robinson tested as the bigger, more explosive jumper — a 37-inch vertical and a 10-foot-4 broad jump at 215 pounds are legitimately elite numbers for a back his size, and they match how he plays: powerful, contact-balanced, and just as comfortable between the tackles as outside. Gibbs, 16 pounds lighter, tested as the straight-line burner, and that shows up on tape as a home-run threat who can turn a five-yard checkdown into a touchdown from anywhere on the field.</p>
<p>Both profiles translated. Robinson led the NFL with 2,298 all-purpose yards in the 2025 season. Gibbs entered 2026 fantasy conversations as a legitimate case for the first overall pick in redraft leagues. Different combine profiles, same conclusion: both are among the two or three best running backs alive right now.</p>
