<h2 id="resolution">How It Resolved</h2>
<p>Robinson signed first: a three-year extension worth up to $75 million with the Falcons, making him the NFL's highest-paid running back at $22.25 million per year. Read the full breakdown of that deal in our <a class="inline" href="https://athletecalculator.com/bijan-robinson-record-extension-highest-paid-rb">Bijan Robinson extension coverage</a>. That deal now sets the number Gibbs and the Lions are working against, along with Colts running back Jonathan Taylor, who was also reportedly eyeing an extension of his own.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Why did Robinson and Gibbs both hold in at the same time?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Both players became extension-eligible in the same offseason as 2023 first-round picks whose rookie deals were running out. Neither wanted to be the one to set the market at a number lower than the other could get, so both held in while their representatives negotiated separately.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What's the difference between a hold-in and a holdout?</div>
<div class="faq-a">A holdout means a player stays away from the team facility entirely. A hold-in means the player reports to camp and is present at meetings and walkthroughs but doesn't participate in practice drills — a lower-risk way to apply pressure in a contract negotiation while staying in shape and avoiding fines.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Whose combine profile was more impressive, Robinson's or Gibbs's?</div>
<div class="faq-a">They're not directly comparable because they tested for different things. Gibbs's 4.36 forty is faster in absolute terms, but Robinson's 37-inch vertical and 10'4" broad jump are more impressive relative to his 215-pound frame. Both profiles are considered elite for the running back position.</div>
</div>
